WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.205
Lesson 1.3.

00:00:02.205 --> 00:00:04.985
Wallets, keys, hardware and multisig.

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:06.625
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:08.593
set up a wallet structure where one

00:00:08.593 --> 00:00:10.670
mistake can't lose everything you own.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.260
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.865
First, the five words: private key, seed

00:00:15.865 --> 00:00:19.405
phrase, hardware wallet, multisig and smart wallet.

00:00:19.405 --> 00:00:23.171
Second, compartmentalising: why one wallet for everything is

00:00:23.171 --> 00:00:26.435
the single biggest structural risk you can take.

00:00:26.435 --> 00:00:29.678
And third, the worked example this whole lesson builds

00:00:29.678 --> 00:00:32.920
to: the three-wallet setup, vault, operator and burner.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.135
Five words.

00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:38.260
Word one: private key.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.855
The authority to sign.

00:00:39.855 --> 00:00:42.741
Whoever has it controls the funds, completely,

00:00:42.741 --> 00:00:45.190
with no exceptions and no override.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.265
It isn't a password you can reset.

00:00:47.265 --> 00:00:49.474
It's closer to the only physical key

00:00:49.474 --> 00:00:51.445
to a vault that has no locksmith.

00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:54.660
Word two: seed phrase.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.947
12 to 24 words that recreate your keys.

00:00:57.947 --> 00:00:59.972
Never type it into a website.

00:00:59.972 --> 00:01:01.517
Never photograph it.

00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:02.722
Never share it.

00:01:02.722 --> 00:01:05.596
No legitimate support team will ever ask for

00:01:05.596 --> 00:01:08.087
it, not once, not for any reason, ever.

00:01:09.387 --> 00:01:12.282
Here's why the checklist says two separate physical

00:01:12.282 --> 00:01:14.642
places, and an honest caveat with it.

00:01:14.642 --> 00:01:17.519
A single copy protects against theft, but not

00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:20.146
against a fire, a flood, or simply losing

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:22.072
the one place you kept it.

00:01:22.072 --> 00:01:25.132
A second copy, somewhere else, protects against that.

00:01:25.132 --> 00:01:28.159
But be precise about what it doesn't do: each

00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:31.121
full copy can recreate every key on its own,

00:01:31.121 --> 00:01:34.544
so a second copy is redundancy against disaster and

00:01:34.544 --> 00:01:37.542
loss, not extra protection against theft.

00:01:37.542 --> 00:01:39.394
Both locations still need to

00:01:39.394 --> 00:01:41.437
be genuinely secure and private.

00:01:42.737 --> 00:01:44.802
Word three: hardware wallet.

00:01:44.802 --> 00:01:46.969
A separate physical device that keeps

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:49.322
your keys off the internet entirely.

00:01:49.322 --> 00:01:51.280
When an app asks you to sign, the

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.756
request travels to the device, you read it

00:01:53.756 --> 00:01:56.289
on the device's own screen, and you confirm

00:01:56.289 --> 00:01:58.432
it with a physical button press.

00:01:58.432 --> 00:02:01.145
Even if the computer you're using is compromised,

00:02:01.145 --> 00:02:03.967
the signing itself happens somewhere it can't reach.

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:08.702
Word four: multisig, from Lesson one point zero.

00:02:08.702 --> 00:02:10.964
Needs M of N signers, for example

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.392
two of three, before it can act.

00:02:13.392 --> 00:02:15.985
Good for large or shared funds, because

00:02:15.985 --> 00:02:18.448
a single compromised key, on its own,

00:02:18.448 --> 00:02:20.847
is no longer enough to lose anything.

00:02:22.147 --> 00:02:24.372
And word five: smart wallets.

00:02:24.372 --> 00:02:29.055
These are contract accounts, not simple key-controlled addresses, which

00:02:29.055 --> 00:02:32.307
means they can add features a basic wallet can't:

00:02:32.307 --> 00:02:35.820
spending limits, social recovery if a device is lost,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.242
and batching several actions into one signature.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:42.427
You'll see these in more depth in Lesson 1.9.

00:02:42.427 --> 00:02:44.557
For now, just know the category exists,

00:02:44.557 --> 00:02:46.741
and that it's a genuinely different kind

00:02:46.741 --> 00:02:48.072
of wallet under the hood.

00:02:49.372 --> 00:02:52.317
Here's why the device matters, not just the key.

00:02:52.317 --> 00:02:54.865
A software wallet keeps your key inside

00:02:54.865 --> 00:02:57.159
an app on your everyday computer or

00:02:57.159 --> 00:02:59.580
phone, the same device you browse the

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.747
internet, open email and install software on.

00:03:02.747 --> 00:03:05.438
A hardware wallet keeps the key on a separate,

00:03:05.438 --> 00:03:09.287
purpose-built device that never touches the internet directly.

00:03:09.287 --> 00:03:11.491
Malware on your computer can potentially

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.297
see a software wallet's key.

00:03:13.297 --> 00:03:15.502
It cannot reach inside a hardware device.

00:03:16.802 --> 00:03:18.995
One real example of why reading the

00:03:18.995 --> 00:03:21.732
device screen matters, not just the app.

00:03:21.732 --> 00:03:24.474
In December of two thousand twenty-three, a

00:03:24.474 --> 00:03:28.027
widely used wallet-connection library was compromised at

00:03:28.027 --> 00:03:31.205
the supply-chain level, briefly showing some users

00:03:31.205 --> 00:03:36.304
malicious transaction requests through otherwise legitimate-looking websites.

00:03:36.304 --> 00:03:38.919
People who read and verified the request on

00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:41.894
their hardware device's own screen caught it.

00:03:41.894 --> 00:03:44.839
People who only glanced at the website did not.

00:03:44.839 --> 00:03:46.976
The device screen is the one thing an

00:03:46.976 --> 00:03:49.844
attacker who's compromised your browser can't fake.

00:03:51.144 --> 00:03:54.346
One more real, current fact on smart wallets:

00:03:54.346 --> 00:03:58.940
the underlying standard, generally called account abstraction, is

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.282
a real and increasingly used part of Ethereum's

00:04:02.282 --> 00:04:04.809
ecosystem, not a future promise.

00:04:04.809 --> 00:04:07.771
It's what lets a wallet require two devices to

00:04:07.771 --> 00:04:11.740
approve a large transaction, or recover access through trusted

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:14.624
contacts instead of a single seed phrase.

00:04:14.624 --> 00:04:17.419
You'll build on this properly in Lesson 1.9.

00:04:18.719 --> 00:04:19.809
Quick check.

00:04:19.809 --> 00:04:22.164
A message from “support” asks for your

00:04:22.164 --> 00:04:24.819
seed phrase to fix a stuck transaction.

00:04:24.819 --> 00:04:25.669
What do you do?

00:04:30.089 --> 00:04:31.824
The answer: nothing.

00:04:31.824 --> 00:04:34.114
It's a scam, every single time.

00:04:34.114 --> 00:04:35.614
Block and report.

00:04:35.614 --> 00:04:38.244
No legitimate support team will ever ask for it.

00:04:39.544 --> 00:04:40.724
Compartmentalise.

00:04:42.024 --> 00:04:46.015
Compartmentalising isn't unique to crypto; it's the same logic

00:04:46.015 --> 00:04:49.119
behind carrying a slim wallet with a little cash

00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:52.286
instead of your entire bank card collection, or a

00:04:52.286 --> 00:04:56.767
business keeping its operating account separate from its reserves.

00:04:56.767 --> 00:04:59.793
On-chain, it matters more, because there's no bank

00:04:59.793 --> 00:05:02.763
to call if the wrong wallet gets compromised.

00:05:02.763 --> 00:05:04.560
The structure has to do the job

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.581
a phone call would do anywhere else.

00:05:07.881 --> 00:05:11.436
Here's the structural risk most beginners never think about.

00:05:11.436 --> 00:05:14.903
One wallet for everything means your life savings, your

00:05:14.903 --> 00:05:18.926
everyday DeFi positions and your experiments with brand-new apps

00:05:18.926 --> 00:05:21.331
all sit behind the exact same key.

00:05:21.331 --> 00:05:23.797
One bad signature on one sketchy new app,

00:05:23.797 --> 00:05:26.856
and everything you own is reachable through it.

00:05:26.856 --> 00:05:29.914
Separate wallets by job, and a compromise in one

00:05:29.914 --> 00:05:32.723
only ever reaches what that one wallet holds.

00:05:34.023 --> 00:05:35.298
The 3-wallet setup.

00:05:36.598 --> 00:05:38.018
Here's the structure.

00:05:38.018 --> 00:05:41.081
The vault holds your long-term holdings, and it

00:05:41.081 --> 00:05:44.188
almost never signs: no DeFi approvals, ever.

00:05:44.188 --> 00:05:47.325
The operator holds your active DeFi positions,

00:05:47.325 --> 00:05:50.696
and only signs with known, verified protocols.

00:05:50.696 --> 00:05:53.011
And the burner holds small amounts for

00:05:53.011 --> 00:05:55.266
new apps, mints and airdrops, and can

00:05:55.266 --> 00:05:58.294
sign anything experimental, because it never holds

00:05:58.294 --> 00:06:00.846
more than you're prepared to lose entirely.

00:06:02.146 --> 00:06:05.176
Money moves down this chain, only as needed.

00:06:05.176 --> 00:06:07.289
From the vault, to the operator, to the

00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:10.071
burner, when you actually need funds in motion.

00:06:10.071 --> 00:06:12.629
And profits sweep back up, from burner to

00:06:12.629 --> 00:06:16.036
operator to vault, once a position's proven itself.

00:06:16.036 --> 00:06:18.163
If the burner gets drained completely,

00:06:18.163 --> 00:06:20.536
you've lost only the burner's balance.

00:06:20.536 --> 00:06:22.916
The vault was never in the blast radius.

00:06:24.216 --> 00:06:27.187
Here's an illustrative example of how someone might

00:06:27.187 --> 00:06:30.001
split their crypto across the three wallets.

00:06:30.001 --> 00:06:31.575
The bulk, say 80%, sits

00:06:31.575 --> 00:06:33.776
in the vault, rarely touched.

00:06:33.776 --> 00:06:38.051
15% sits in the operator, in active, known positions.

00:06:38.051 --> 00:06:40.795
And 5%, or less, sits in the burner, small

00:06:40.795 --> 00:06:44.541
enough that losing all of it wouldn't change anything.

00:06:44.541 --> 00:06:47.253
Your own split depends on your total and your

00:06:47.253 --> 00:06:49.966
comfort with risk, but the shape, most in the

00:06:49.966 --> 00:06:53.091
vault, least in the burner, holds regardless of size.

00:06:54.391 --> 00:06:57.389
Here's the compartmentalising habit as four steps,

00:06:57.389 --> 00:06:59.276
so it's not just a diagram.

00:06:59.276 --> 00:07:01.667
Decide a position's job first: is

00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:04.901
this long-term, active, or experimental?

00:07:04.901 --> 00:07:06.257
Route it to the matching

00:07:06.257 --> 00:07:08.346
wallet, never the convenient one.

00:07:08.346 --> 00:07:10.981
Sign only what that wallet's job allows.

00:07:10.981 --> 00:07:13.518
And sweep profits back up the chain once a

00:07:13.518 --> 00:07:16.999
position's proven itself, so the operator and burner don't

00:07:16.999 --> 00:07:19.831
quietly grow past what they're supposed to hold.

00:07:21.131 --> 00:07:23.799
Notice the device column too: vault and

00:07:23.799 --> 00:07:26.333
operator on hardware, or the vault in

00:07:26.333 --> 00:07:28.300
a multisig for real size.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.263
The burner can stay a simple hot

00:07:30.263 --> 00:07:33.360
wallet, because it's designed to be disposable.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.415
The structure does the protecting.

00:07:35.415 --> 00:07:37.240
You don't have to remember to be

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.783
careful every single time, because the wallet

00:07:39.783 --> 00:07:41.940
you're using already limits the damage.

00:07:43.239 --> 00:07:46.264
Here's a habit worth naming out loud: labelling

00:07:46.264 --> 00:07:49.163
your wallets by name, right inside the wallet

00:07:49.163 --> 00:07:51.795
app, not just by remembering which is

00:07:51.795 --> 00:07:55.551
which. “Vault,” “Operator,” “Burner,” or whatever names you choose,

00:07:55.551 --> 00:07:58.005
visible every time you open the app. It

00:07:58.005 --> 00:07:59.300
sounds small. In

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.104
practice, it's the difference between pausing to

00:08:02.104 --> 00:08:04.621
check which wallet you're signing from, and

00:08:04.621 --> 00:08:06.910
signing on autopilot from the wrong one.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.300
Quick check.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:10.727
Why keep the vault wallet

00:08:10.727 --> 00:08:12.592
completely free of DeFi approvals?

00:08:17.012 --> 00:08:20.597
The answer: approvals let a contract move your tokens.

00:08:20.597 --> 00:08:23.675
With none granted, a compromised protocol has no

00:08:23.675 --> 00:08:25.622
path to reach the vault at all.

00:08:26.922 --> 00:08:29.187
One last thing worth saying plainly.

00:08:29.187 --> 00:08:30.827
None of this structure has to

00:08:30.827 --> 00:08:32.822
be built in a single afternoon.

00:08:32.822 --> 00:08:35.624
Set up the burner first, since it's the lowest-stakes

00:08:35.624 --> 00:08:38.777
piece and the one you'll use constantly while learning.

00:08:38.777 --> 00:08:40.616
Add the operator once you have

00:08:40.616 --> 00:08:43.051
real positions worth separating out.

00:08:43.051 --> 00:08:45.180
And build the vault, with a hardware

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.770
device or a multisig, once there's something

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.681
in it worth that extra step.

00:08:49.681 --> 00:08:52.771
The order matters less than eventually having all three.

00:08:54.072 --> 00:08:55.257
Last check.

00:08:55.257 --> 00:08:58.542
What does a two-of-three multisig actually protect against?

00:09:02.962 --> 00:09:06.152
The answer: a single lost or compromised key.

00:09:06.152 --> 00:09:08.635
Two signers are still needed to move funds,

00:09:08.635 --> 00:09:10.272
so one key alone can't do it.

00:09:11.572 --> 00:09:13.032
Here's your checklist.

00:09:13.032 --> 00:09:14.567
Do it now, for real.

00:09:14.567 --> 00:09:16.684
First, your seed phrase is stored

00:09:16.684 --> 00:09:19.537
offline, in two separate physical places.

00:09:19.537 --> 00:09:21.475
Second, your vault wallet has

00:09:21.475 --> 00:09:23.777
never approved a DeFi contract.

00:09:23.777 --> 00:09:25.115
Third, you have a separate

00:09:25.115 --> 00:09:26.952
burner wallet for anything new.

00:09:26.952 --> 00:09:29.794
And fourth, you read every signing prompt on the

00:09:29.794 --> 00:09:32.752
hardware device's own screen, not just the website.

00:09:34.051 --> 00:09:35.296
Let's recap.

00:09:35.296 --> 00:09:37.666
Five words: private key, seed phrase,

00:09:37.666 --> 00:09:40.461
hardware wallet, multisig, smart wallet.

00:09:40.461 --> 00:09:43.682
Compartmentalise: separate wallets by job, so one

00:09:43.682 --> 00:09:46.301
compromise reaches only one of them.

00:09:46.301 --> 00:09:49.910
And the three-wallet setup: vault, operator, burner, money

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:52.846
moving down as needed, profits sweeping back up.

00:09:54.146 --> 00:09:56.661
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:09:56.661 --> 00:09:58.854
You now have the whole lesson in your

00:09:58.854 --> 00:10:02.202
head: the five words, why compartmentalising matters, and

00:10:02.202 --> 00:10:05.388
the three-wallet setup that puts it into practice.

00:10:05.388 --> 00:10:09.810
Next up, Lesson 1.4: Tokens, approvals and allowances.
